Step-by-Step Solution: Forensics Git 1
Step 1: Decompress the Disk Image

The disk image was provided as a compressed file disk.img.gz. When attempting to decompress it with gunzip, an "unexpected end of file" error occurred initially, but using gunzip -v (verbose mode) successfully decompressed the file to disk.img.
Step 2: Analyze the Disk Image

Using the file command on disk.img revealed it's a DOS/MBR boot sector with three partitions:

    Partition 1: Linux filesystem, startsector 2048 (614400 sectors)

    Partition 2: Linux swap, startsector 616448 (524288 sectors)

    Partition 3: Linux filesystem, startsector 1140736 (956416 sectors)

Step 3: Mount the Partitions

Since the Git repository was likely on a data partition, we mounted the third partition using the offset calculation (sector size is typically 512 bytes):

    Offset = startsector × 512 = 1140736 × 512

Command used: sudo mount -o loop,offset=$((1140736 * 512)) disk.img /mnt/forensics3
Step 4: Explore the Mounted Filesystem

After mounting, we explored the directory structure and found a home directory for a user "ctf-player":
text

/mnt/forensics3/home/ctf-player/Code/secrets/

Step 5: Locate the Git Repository

Using the find command to search for Git repositories:
bash

find /mnt/forensics3 -name ".git" -type d

This revealed a Git repository at: /mnt/forensics3/home/ctf-player/Code/secrets/.git
Step 6: Examine Git History

Navigating to the repository and checking the commit history with git log --oneline showed two commits:

    5fb8194 (HEAD → master) "Remove flag" (later commit)

    177789a "Add flag" (earlier commit)

Step 7: Access the Earlier Commit

The hint "How can you checkout the files of a previous commit?" guided us to check out the commit where the flag was added:
bash

git checkout 177789a

This put us in a "detached HEAD" state at the commit that added the flag.
Step 8: Find the Flag File

Listing the files in this commit revealed:

    flag.txt - a text file containing the flag

    .git - the Git repository directory

Step 9: Read the Flag

Finally, reading the contents of flag.txt would reveal the flag.
